WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:08.069
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:09.979
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.979 --> 00:00:12.929
Minds by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.939 --> 00:00:14.709
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.709 --> 00:00:17.209
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:19.349
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:21.199
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.719 --> 00:00:24.869
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:27.399
going to talk about one of the most

00:00:27.409 --> 00:00:31.079
divisive, bitter, and emotionally charged

00:00:31.109 --> 00:00:33.549
periods in modern British history:

00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:37.253
the miners' strike of the mid 1980s.

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:41.579
This is a story about coal, about class,

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:44.099
about power, and about what happens

00:00:44.229 --> 00:00:46.449
when a government and a community

00:00:46.639 --> 00:00:49.589
decide that neither side will back down.

00:00:50.139 --> 00:00:52.279
So, let’s not waste a minute,

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:54.399
and get right into it.

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:59.929
It was the 8th of April, 2013.

00:01:00.959 --> 00:01:03.099
The family of Margaret Thatcher,

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:05.459
the former British Prime Minister

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:07.719
who had governed the country from

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:12.559
1979 to 1990, announced that she had

00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:16.379
died of a stroke at the age of 87.

00:01:17.519 --> 00:01:21.219
Now, for most people, the death of an

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:24.769
elderly woman, especially someone who was

00:01:24.769 --> 00:01:26.939
one of the longest serving British Prime

00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:30.609
Ministers, well it would be a sad moment.

00:01:30.989 --> 00:01:34.769
A moment of reflection, of thanks for

00:01:34.769 --> 00:01:37.559
their decade-plus of public service.

00:01:38.769 --> 00:01:40.799
And, when Margaret Thatcher

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:43.639
died, tributes certainly did

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:46.029
pour in from around the world.

00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:49.609
The right-leaning Daily Mail ran

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.089
the headline: "The Woman Who Saved

00:01:52.129 --> 00:01:54.509
Britain", while The Daily Express

00:01:54.779 --> 00:01:56.159
went with a reference to her

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:59.149
nickname, “Farewell, Iron Lady”.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:02.849
Her supporters described her as the

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:05.279
greatest peacetime prime minister

00:02:05.589 --> 00:02:08.819
the UK had ever had, a woman who

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:11.319
made hard decisions and turned

00:02:11.469 --> 00:02:13.189
the country’s economy around.

00:02:14.585 --> 00:02:17.635
Not everyone was so complimentary.

00:02:18.995 --> 00:02:21.625
In the coal belt of northern England,

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:24.815
a former miner lit up an enormous

00:02:24.815 --> 00:02:27.235
cigar and said he was looking

00:02:27.235 --> 00:02:29.595
forward to some celebratory drinks.

00:02:30.485 --> 00:02:32.455
Street parties broke out in

00:02:32.455 --> 00:02:35.035
Glasgow, Liverpool, Bristol,

00:02:35.205 --> 00:02:37.795
Leeds, Cardiff, and Belfast.

00:02:38.615 --> 00:02:41.205
The Socialist Worker newspaper announced

00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:44.245
the death with the headline “REJOICE”,

00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:47.375
and enticed its readers with the prospect

00:02:47.385 --> 00:02:50.535
of a “Thatcher’s Dead Special Pullout”.

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:54.605
And thanks to a coordinated online

00:02:54.605 --> 00:02:58.275
campaign, the song "Ding Dong! The

00:02:58.275 --> 00:03:01.205
Witch Is Dead," from The Wizard of Oz,

00:03:01.673 --> 00:03:05.053
shot to number two in the UK charts,

00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:08.163
forcing the BBC to decide whether to

00:03:08.213 --> 00:03:10.853
play it or not on the weekly chart show.

00:03:12.113 --> 00:03:14.273
The regional secretary of the National

00:03:14.273 --> 00:03:16.463
Union of Mineworkers said that he

00:03:16.463 --> 00:03:18.743
was having a drink to celebrate her

00:03:18.753 --> 00:03:22.237
death, adding that it was also his 70th

00:03:22.237 --> 00:03:25.553
birthday that day, and this made it one

00:03:25.583 --> 00:03:28.483
of the best birthdays he'd ever had.

00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:32.403
Now, although Margaret Thatcher is one

00:03:32.403 --> 00:03:35.563
of if not the central characters of the

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:39.013
miners’ strike, this isn’t all about her.

00:03:39.763 --> 00:03:42.073
We did actually make an episode about her

00:03:42.073 --> 00:03:45.573
life, it’s number 59 if you’re interested.

00:03:46.163 --> 00:03:48.313
But let me give you a little

00:03:48.323 --> 00:03:51.333
background, or a reminder, of who

00:03:51.343 --> 00:03:54.143
Margaret Thatcher was, as you can’t

00:03:54.193 --> 00:03:56.303
understand the miners’ strike without

00:03:56.353 --> 00:03:58.043
understanding Margaret Thatcher.

00:03:58.973 --> 00:04:02.173
So, she was born Margaret Roberts,

00:04:02.433 --> 00:04:04.633
the younger daughter of a grocer

00:04:04.913 --> 00:04:06.853
from the town of Grantham in

00:04:06.853 --> 00:04:09.443
Lincolnshire, in the English Midlands.

00:04:10.393 --> 00:04:13.523
She studied chemistry at Oxford, worked

00:04:13.563 --> 00:04:16.013
briefly as a chemist before becoming a

00:04:16.053 --> 00:04:18.653
barrister, which is a type of lawyer.

00:04:19.633 --> 00:04:23.893
In 1950, at the age of 24, she decided

00:04:23.943 --> 00:04:26.743
to enter politics as a Conservative.

00:04:28.003 --> 00:04:30.963
She rose through the party ranks and

00:04:31.243 --> 00:04:34.843
by 1975 she had become not just its

00:04:34.863 --> 00:04:38.083
leader, but the first woman to lead

00:04:38.103 --> 00:04:40.143
a major British political party.

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:44.673
Four years later, in 1979, she won

00:04:44.723 --> 00:04:46.873
the general election and became the

00:04:46.873 --> 00:04:49.603
first female British Prime Minister.

00:04:51.013 --> 00:04:52.693
Clearly, this was a

00:04:52.693 --> 00:04:54.093
remarkable achievement.

00:04:54.663 --> 00:04:57.263
And even her greatest critics

00:04:57.553 --> 00:04:59.063
would have to admit that she

00:04:59.063 --> 00:05:02.083
was a remarkable woman: fiercely

00:05:02.083 --> 00:05:04.813
intelligent, intensely ideological,

00:05:05.113 --> 00:05:08.373
and utterly unafraid of confrontation.

00:05:09.582 --> 00:05:11.872
And in terms of her political

00:05:11.892 --> 00:05:14.552
beliefs, she believed in free

00:05:14.562 --> 00:05:16.792
markets and private enterprise.

00:05:17.532 --> 00:05:20.382
She believed the state should be strong

00:05:20.392 --> 00:05:23.172
when it came to law and order, but

00:05:23.472 --> 00:05:25.542
much less involved in the economy.

00:05:26.622 --> 00:05:29.162
She was deeply suspicious of trade

00:05:29.172 --> 00:05:32.702
unions, which she saw as obstacles

00:05:32.762 --> 00:05:35.522
to economic reform and, at their most

00:05:35.522 --> 00:05:39.132
extreme, as threats to democracy itself.

00:05:40.382 --> 00:05:43.412
It is perhaps unsurprising that

00:05:43.412 --> 00:05:45.622
she had come to these conclusions.

00:05:46.842 --> 00:05:50.142
The Britain she inherited, the Britain of

00:05:50.142 --> 00:05:54.282
the late 1970s, was not exactly thriving.

00:05:55.532 --> 00:05:58.792
In 1979 the country had been through

00:05:58.792 --> 00:06:01.702
a turbulent decade of strikes,

00:06:02.132 --> 00:06:05.012
power cuts, and industrial unrest.

00:06:05.652 --> 00:06:08.522
The unions were powerful, so powerful,

00:06:08.522 --> 00:06:10.782
in fact, that a miners’ strike in

00:06:10.782 --> 00:06:14.362
1974 had effectively brought down the

00:06:14.362 --> 00:06:16.552
Conservative government, and paved

00:06:16.562 --> 00:06:18.982
the way for Labour to take power.

00:06:20.232 --> 00:06:22.872
Thatcher had watched all of this.

00:06:23.392 --> 00:06:26.362
She had no intention of being

00:06:26.382 --> 00:06:28.072
brought down the same way.

00:06:29.532 --> 00:06:31.522
The problem was that the coal

00:06:31.532 --> 00:06:33.202
mining industry in the UK

00:06:33.582 --> 00:06:35.492
was still large and powerful.

00:06:36.552 --> 00:06:40.802
In 1983, there were over 170 coal

00:06:40.802 --> 00:06:42.592
mines operating across Britain,

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:46.422
employing almost 200,000 miners.

00:06:47.402 --> 00:06:49.492
They were all state owned,

00:06:49.772 --> 00:06:51.782
after being nationalised right

00:06:51.842 --> 00:06:53.045
after the Second World War.

00:06:54.257 --> 00:06:57.217
These mines were all over the country,

00:06:57.497 --> 00:06:59.677
but the largest concentrations

00:06:59.867 --> 00:07:01.567
were in the Midlands, the

00:07:01.567 --> 00:07:04.487
North, South Wales and Scotland.

00:07:05.937 --> 00:07:08.017
And these mines were

00:07:08.017 --> 00:07:09.997
not just places of work.

00:07:11.087 --> 00:07:14.357
In many pit villages, as they were called,

00:07:14.787 --> 00:07:18.137
the mine was the only real employer,

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:21.297
the only option for a stable job.

00:07:22.047 --> 00:07:24.957
The mine supported the entire community,

00:07:25.307 --> 00:07:29.397
so even if 200,000 miners might not

00:07:29.397 --> 00:07:32.457
sound like all that much, these jobs

00:07:32.547 --> 00:07:34.927
provided the incomes that supported

00:07:34.927 --> 00:07:37.397
families and entire communities.

00:07:38.437 --> 00:07:40.727
But the industry had been

00:07:40.727 --> 00:07:42.307
in long-term decline.

00:07:43.147 --> 00:07:45.007
Cheaper oil and natural gas

00:07:45.387 --> 00:07:47.207
had reduced demand for coal.

00:07:48.037 --> 00:07:50.187
Many mines were running at a

00:07:50.197 --> 00:07:52.657
loss and were being heavily

00:07:52.657 --> 00:07:54.117
subsidised by the government.

00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:57.637
Margaret Thatcher, with her deep

00:07:57.667 --> 00:08:00.127
commitment to and belief in free

00:08:00.127 --> 00:08:03.087
markets, well, this went against

00:08:03.237 --> 00:08:04.937
everything she believed in.

00:08:05.897 --> 00:08:08.817
She wanted to end those subsidies,

00:08:09.127 --> 00:08:11.877
and close the unprofitable mines.

00:08:13.347 --> 00:08:16.867
In 1983, she appointed a new chairman

00:08:17.157 --> 00:08:20.177
of the National Coal Board, the NCB,

00:08:20.317 --> 00:08:23.137
the state body that ran the mines.

00:08:24.327 --> 00:08:27.797
His name was Ian MacGregor, and he

00:08:27.797 --> 00:08:29.707
was a Scottish-American businessman

00:08:30.077 --> 00:08:32.497
who had previously restructured the

00:08:32.497 --> 00:08:35.017
steel industry by closing plants

00:08:35.237 --> 00:08:37.507
and cutting the workforce in half.

00:08:38.307 --> 00:08:40.267
The message was not subtle.

00:08:41.157 --> 00:08:43.197
The same was coming for coal.

00:08:44.247 --> 00:08:46.327
On the other side of the coming

00:08:46.347 --> 00:08:49.147
confrontation was a man called Arthur

00:08:49.147 --> 00:08:51.777
Scargill, who was the president of the

00:08:51.787 --> 00:08:55.357
NUM, the National Union of Mineworkers.

00:08:56.307 --> 00:08:58.927
He was a militant trade unionist

00:08:58.937 --> 00:09:01.917
from Yorkshire, deeply socialist in

00:09:01.917 --> 00:09:04.727
his politics, and utterly convinced

00:09:04.937 --> 00:09:07.347
that the government's real aim was

00:09:07.347 --> 00:09:10.237
not simply economic efficiency but

00:09:10.517 --> 00:09:13.177
the complete destruction of both

00:09:13.207 --> 00:09:16.137
the coal industry and the unions.

00:09:17.457 --> 00:09:21.617
So, the stage was set: an industry that

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:23.847
supported hundreds of thousands of people

00:09:24.127 --> 00:09:26.957
up and down the country, and a Prime

00:09:26.957 --> 00:09:30.097
Minister who looked set on destroying it.

00:09:31.237 --> 00:09:33.267
The spark that lit the fire

00:09:33.737 --> 00:09:36.801
came in early March 1984.

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:40.867
On the 1st of March, the National

00:09:40.897 --> 00:09:42.927
Coal Board announced the closure

00:09:43.087 --> 00:09:45.297
of a mine in South Yorkshire.

00:09:46.317 --> 00:09:47.837
The miners there walked

00:09:47.837 --> 00:09:49.417
out almost immediately in

00:09:49.417 --> 00:09:51.827
protest; they went on strike.

00:09:52.727 --> 00:09:55.717
Then, on the 6th of March, MacGregor

00:09:55.997 --> 00:09:58.956
announced plans to close 20 pits across

00:09:58.956 --> 00:10:02.996
the country, with the loss of 20,000 jobs.

00:10:04.496 --> 00:10:07.226
Scargill insisted the real figure was

00:10:07.236 --> 00:10:09.656
far higher, claiming the government

00:10:09.906 --> 00:10:12.466
planned to shut more than 70 pits.

00:10:13.856 --> 00:10:15.546
The government denied it.

00:10:16.686 --> 00:10:19.966
But…Scargill was not making it up.

00:10:20.906 --> 00:10:23.776
More than three decades later, government

00:10:23.786 --> 00:10:25.696
papers were released showing that

00:10:25.696 --> 00:10:28.766
MacGregor did indeed have plans to

00:10:28.766 --> 00:10:32.326
close around 75 pits within three years.

00:10:33.346 --> 00:10:35.856
On the 12th of March, Scargill,

00:10:35.986 --> 00:10:38.846
the union boss, united the growing

00:10:38.856 --> 00:10:41.476
regional strikes into a national

00:10:41.506 --> 00:10:43.616
action, declaring the National

00:10:43.616 --> 00:10:46.056
Union of Mineworkers’ full support.

00:10:47.226 --> 00:10:50.206
Crucially, and controversially, he

00:10:50.206 --> 00:10:52.806
did this without holding a national

00:10:52.816 --> 00:10:56.346
ballot, a vote of all the union members.

00:10:57.406 --> 00:10:59.386
Scargill argued that a

00:10:59.386 --> 00:11:01.376
national vote wasn't needed.

00:11:02.356 --> 00:11:04.956
In the trade union movement there was

00:11:04.956 --> 00:11:07.606
a strong principle that you never cross

00:11:07.646 --> 00:11:10.236
a picket line — that if your fellow

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:13.196
workers are already on strike, you don't

00:11:13.196 --> 00:11:15.696
walk past them and go to work as usual.

00:11:17.096 --> 00:11:19.236
Miners in Yorkshire and other

00:11:19.246 --> 00:11:21.146
areas had already walked out.

00:11:21.706 --> 00:11:24.011
And Scargill's argument was that

00:11:24.086 --> 00:11:25.726
miners everywhere else in the

00:11:25.726 --> 00:11:27.936
country were simply honour-bound

00:11:27.946 --> 00:11:30.676
to join them, vote or no vote.

00:11:31.906 --> 00:11:34.436
His critics, including some on

00:11:34.446 --> 00:11:37.106
the left, argued that calling a

00:11:37.106 --> 00:11:39.696
national strike without a vote was

00:11:39.696 --> 00:11:42.446
fundamentally undemocratic; he couldn’t

00:11:42.446 --> 00:11:44.826
just tell union members to strike.

00:11:45.106 --> 00:11:47.220
It needed to be put to a vote.

00:11:48.416 --> 00:11:50.256
So, what happened?

00:11:50.886 --> 00:11:53.886
Well, around 140,000

00:11:53.886 --> 00:11:55.756
miners went on strike.

00:11:56.399 --> 00:11:58.706
But this wasn’t every miner,

00:11:58.786 --> 00:12:00.906
not every member of the union.

00:12:01.856 --> 00:12:04.216
Miners in Nottinghamshire and the

00:12:04.216 --> 00:12:06.565
Midlands largely refused to join,

00:12:06.985 --> 00:12:09.005
as most had voted against the

00:12:09.005 --> 00:12:11.425
strike in previous local ballots.

00:12:12.375 --> 00:12:15.615
Now, before we go further, it's worth

00:12:15.615 --> 00:12:18.355
clarifying what going on strike actually

00:12:18.355 --> 00:12:20.835
meant, from a practical point of view.

00:12:22.125 --> 00:12:25.625
Simply put, it meant no wages, no pay.

00:12:26.315 --> 00:12:29.065
A miner on strike received nothing

00:12:29.325 --> 00:12:30.785
from the National Coal Board,

00:12:31.065 --> 00:12:33.115
obviously, since he wasn't working.

00:12:33.765 --> 00:12:36.685
And strike pay from the union itself

00:12:36.945 --> 00:12:39.905
was minimal, partly because the

00:12:39.925 --> 00:12:43.165
NUM's funds were limited and, as the

00:12:43.165 --> 00:12:45.155
months went on, partly because those

00:12:45.165 --> 00:12:47.645
funds were frozen by the courts.

00:12:48.535 --> 00:12:51.635
So the men who went out and stayed

00:12:51.635 --> 00:12:54.655
out for a year were, in many cases,

00:12:54.935 --> 00:12:57.435
entirely dependent on the goodwill

00:12:57.435 --> 00:12:59.525
of others to feed their families.

00:13:00.615 --> 00:13:02.305
Soup kitchens were set

00:13:02.305 --> 00:13:04.115
up in mining communities.

00:13:04.405 --> 00:13:07.055
Support groups sent food parcels.

00:13:07.285 --> 00:13:09.845
Local businesses gave what they could.

00:13:10.575 --> 00:13:12.735
Solidarity collections were organised

00:13:12.735 --> 00:13:14.825
across the country and beyond.

00:13:15.725 --> 00:13:18.085
The reality was that these men,

00:13:18.455 --> 00:13:20.895
men who had worked hard physical

00:13:20.895 --> 00:13:23.355
jobs underground for their entire

00:13:23.395 --> 00:13:25.585
adult lives were going hungry,

00:13:25.985 --> 00:13:28.355
along with their wives and children.

00:13:29.405 --> 00:13:31.295
Now, with that said,

00:13:31.725 --> 00:13:33.485
what was the strategy?

00:13:33.772 --> 00:13:35.195
What were the miners

00:13:35.405 --> 00:13:37.055
actually trying to achieve?

00:13:38.525 --> 00:13:41.425
The plan, broadly, was to cause an

00:13:41.465 --> 00:13:44.585
energy crisis severe enough to force

00:13:44.585 --> 00:13:46.745
the government to the negotiating table,

00:13:47.065 --> 00:13:49.895
and stop the mines from being closed.

00:13:50.885 --> 00:13:53.055
Britain's economy still depended

00:13:53.135 --> 00:13:56.025
heavily on coal-fired power stations.

00:13:56.525 --> 00:13:59.075
If the mines stopped producing coal,

00:13:59.295 --> 00:14:01.725
if supplies to those power stations

00:14:02.025 --> 00:14:05.345
could be cut off or disrupted, then the

00:14:05.345 --> 00:14:07.205
lights would go out across the country,

00:14:07.635 --> 00:14:10.695
industry would grind to a halt, and

00:14:10.745 --> 00:14:12.785
the government would have no choice

00:14:12.855 --> 00:14:14.935
but to make concessions to the miners.

00:14:16.085 --> 00:14:19.015
And this was not a fantasy;

00:14:19.395 --> 00:14:20.525
it had worked before.

00:14:21.295 --> 00:14:25.025
In 1972, Scargill had used exactly

00:14:25.025 --> 00:14:28.265
this strategy, using mass pickets to

00:14:28.265 --> 00:14:30.525
shut down a key location in Birmingham,

00:14:30.915 --> 00:14:33.055
and it had forced the Conservative

00:14:33.055 --> 00:14:35.375
government of Edward Heath to give

00:14:35.375 --> 00:14:37.505
the miners a significant pay rise.

00:14:38.115 --> 00:14:41.425
And it had worked again in 1974,

00:14:41.935 --> 00:14:43.565
when a miners' strike brought

00:14:43.595 --> 00:14:45.475
down Heath's government entirely.

00:14:46.975 --> 00:14:49.115
What Thatcher had done, with

00:14:49.145 --> 00:14:52.225
great deliberation, was make sure

00:14:52.345 --> 00:14:54.265
it could not work a third time.

00:14:55.175 --> 00:14:57.865
Her government had spent years preparing

00:14:58.045 --> 00:15:00.155
for exactly this confrontation.

00:15:01.155 --> 00:15:02.395
They had stockpiled

00:15:02.465 --> 00:15:04.165
coal at power stations.

00:15:04.745 --> 00:15:06.415
They had converted some plants

00:15:06.645 --> 00:15:08.315
to run on heavy fuel oil.

00:15:08.845 --> 00:15:10.665
They had arranged fleets of road

00:15:10.665 --> 00:15:13.115
hauliers to move coal by lorry.

00:15:14.445 --> 00:15:18.105
By 1984, she had around six

00:15:18.105 --> 00:15:19.505
months of coal reserves.

00:15:20.165 --> 00:15:22.065
And her strategy was

00:15:22.065 --> 00:15:24.765
simple: outlast the miners.

00:15:25.855 --> 00:15:28.205
The strike that followed was one of

00:15:28.205 --> 00:15:31.285
the most bitter and violent episodes of

00:15:31.285 --> 00:15:33.865
industrial conflict in British history.

00:15:35.025 --> 00:15:37.195
The miners used a tactic that had

00:15:37.195 --> 00:15:40.135
served them well in the early 1970s:

00:15:40.535 --> 00:15:43.215
something called the flying picket.

00:15:44.115 --> 00:15:46.815
What this involved was large groups of

00:15:46.815 --> 00:15:49.365
striking miners who would travel quickly

00:15:49.365 --> 00:15:51.825
to a mine or industrial site that was

00:15:51.825 --> 00:15:55.315
still operational, overwhelm any police

00:15:55.345 --> 00:15:58.205
presence, and shut it down completely.

00:15:58.975 --> 00:16:01.125
The problem was, the government

00:16:01.325 --> 00:16:03.965
and the police suspected that this

00:16:03.965 --> 00:16:05.795
was exactly what they would do.

00:16:07.015 --> 00:16:10.115
And in 1984, they were ready.

00:16:11.075 --> 00:16:13.465
Police were deployed in enormous

00:16:13.465 --> 00:16:15.865
numbers to key mining sites.

00:16:16.475 --> 00:16:19.595
Motorway checkpoints were set up to turn

00:16:19.605 --> 00:16:22.304
back miners travelling to join pickets.

00:16:23.195 --> 00:16:26.235
The sheer scale of policing was something

00:16:26.235 --> 00:16:28.315
many people had never seen before in

00:16:28.315 --> 00:16:31.685
Britain, and it led to confrontations that

00:16:31.685 --> 00:16:34.155
were sometimes extraordinarily violent.

00:16:35.535 --> 00:16:38.165
The most dramatic of these came

00:16:38.175 --> 00:16:41.685
in June, 1984, at a plant near

00:16:41.685 --> 00:16:43.875
Rotherham in South Yorkshire.

00:16:45.575 --> 00:16:48.905
Scargill wanted to stop any materials

00:16:48.945 --> 00:16:51.155
from leaving the plant and reaching

00:16:51.155 --> 00:16:54.185
the steel works in Scunthorpe, as it

00:16:54.185 --> 00:16:56.595
would disrupt steel production and

00:16:56.595 --> 00:16:58.985
ratchet up pressure on the government.

00:17:00.465 --> 00:17:02.445
What happened next is remembered

00:17:02.745 --> 00:17:05.005
as the Battle of Orgreave.

00:17:06.215 --> 00:17:10.195
Around 5,000 miners faced roughly the

00:17:10.195 --> 00:17:12.725
same number of police, including officers

00:17:12.725 --> 00:17:16.065
on horseback and others with large dogs.

00:17:17.155 --> 00:17:19.605
And the confrontation became one

00:17:19.615 --> 00:17:22.005
of the most iconic and contested

00:17:22.005 --> 00:17:23.905
moments of the entire strike.

00:17:25.175 --> 00:17:28.205
Police on horseback charged into the crowd

00:17:28.205 --> 00:17:30.776
of strikers with their truncheons drawn.

00:17:31.641 --> 00:17:34.405
Truncheons, by the way, are those short,

00:17:34.815 --> 00:17:37.485
thick sticks that the police often carry.

00:17:38.775 --> 00:17:40.865
Of course, there were casualties.

00:17:41.745 --> 00:17:44.335
Fifty-one striking miners and

00:17:44.335 --> 00:17:46.965
72 police officers were injured.

00:17:47.789 --> 00:17:50.295
Scargill himself was arrested

00:17:50.585 --> 00:17:52.075
and photographed being led

00:17:52.095 --> 00:17:54.035
away with a bloodied head.

00:17:55.235 --> 00:17:57.725
Ninety-five miners were arrested

00:17:58.015 --> 00:18:00.025
and charged with something called

00:18:00.105 --> 00:18:03.325
“riot”, which at the time carried

00:18:03.325 --> 00:18:06.025
the possibility of a life sentence.

00:18:07.515 --> 00:18:10.205
Naturally, it was the biggest news item

00:18:10.205 --> 00:18:14.535
that day, but bafflingly, when the BBC

00:18:14.545 --> 00:18:17.345
broadcast it, it showed the footage of

00:18:17.345 --> 00:18:19.935
the confrontation in the wrong order.

00:18:20.515 --> 00:18:23.005
It showed the miners appearing to

00:18:23.005 --> 00:18:25.605
attack the police first, when in fact

00:18:25.945 --> 00:18:28.195
it was the police that charged first,

00:18:28.475 --> 00:18:31.255
it was the authorities that appeared

00:18:31.475 --> 00:18:33.075
to have provoked the situation.

00:18:34.285 --> 00:18:36.975
Before “fake news” was even a term,

00:18:37.385 --> 00:18:40.275
many miners and their supporters felt

00:18:40.575 --> 00:18:42.835
they had been deliberately framed by

00:18:42.835 --> 00:18:45.175
the national broadcaster, deliberately

00:18:45.185 --> 00:18:47.535
portrayed to have started the fight,

00:18:47.925 --> 00:18:50.305
when many miners believed the police

00:18:50.635 --> 00:18:52.665
had in fact initiated the violence.

00:18:53.975 --> 00:18:56.575
The BBC later said it was a

00:18:56.605 --> 00:18:59.005
simple production mistake, rather

00:18:59.005 --> 00:19:01.435
than some sort of institutional

00:19:01.465 --> 00:19:04.705
bias, but the damage was done.

00:19:06.195 --> 00:19:08.475
As the strike continued into the

00:19:08.485 --> 00:19:11.385
summer and then the autumn, life

00:19:11.475 --> 00:19:13.585
became increasingly tough for the

00:19:13.585 --> 00:19:15.975
miners, and for their communities.

00:19:17.365 --> 00:19:18.655
They didn’t give up.

00:19:19.155 --> 00:19:21.155
But the government was

00:19:21.155 --> 00:19:22.415
doing the arithmetic.

00:19:23.085 --> 00:19:25.425
Coal was still reaching the power

00:19:25.425 --> 00:19:28.445
stations, partly from stockpiles, partly

00:19:28.445 --> 00:19:30.025
from the mines that had never stopped

00:19:30.025 --> 00:19:33.275
working, and partly from imported coal.

00:19:34.155 --> 00:19:36.275
There would be no power cuts.

00:19:37.255 --> 00:19:39.075
In terms of public support,

00:19:39.495 --> 00:19:41.635
the public increasingly began

00:19:41.635 --> 00:19:43.405
to side with the government

00:19:43.755 --> 00:19:45.815
rather than the striking miners.

00:19:47.255 --> 00:19:49.095
At the beginning of the strike,

00:19:49.485 --> 00:19:52.615
support was split relatively equally

00:19:52.665 --> 00:19:54.905
between the miners and the employers,

00:19:54.955 --> 00:19:57.975
or the government, but by December

00:19:57.995 --> 00:20:01.425
of 1984, nine months into the strike,

00:20:01.995 --> 00:20:04.935
51% of the public said that they

00:20:04.935 --> 00:20:07.815
supported the employers, and support

00:20:07.825 --> 00:20:11.115
for the miners had dropped to 26%.

00:20:12.485 --> 00:20:15.065
And almost every major newspaper

00:20:15.585 --> 00:20:18.075
took an “anti-miners” stance,

00:20:18.225 --> 00:20:19.925
which certainly didn’t help.

00:20:21.055 --> 00:20:22.895
Things were not looking good.

00:20:23.765 --> 00:20:26.945
And as winter set in, more and

00:20:26.945 --> 00:20:29.145
more miners, facing months without

00:20:29.145 --> 00:20:31.355
pay and with families to feed,

00:20:31.835 --> 00:20:33.595
well, perfectly understandably,

00:20:33.915 --> 00:20:35.835
they began drifting back to work.

00:20:37.025 --> 00:20:39.835
And there didn’t seem to be any way out

00:20:40.055 --> 00:20:42.195
for the miners that were still on strike.

00:20:42.945 --> 00:20:45.585
The union’s assets were still frozen.

00:20:46.125 --> 00:20:47.925
Scargill's attempt to shut down

00:20:47.925 --> 00:20:50.515
the plant at Orgreave had failed.

00:20:51.045 --> 00:20:52.875
The strategy of flying

00:20:52.875 --> 00:20:55.165
pickets had been neutralised.

00:20:56.125 --> 00:20:58.705
There was one potential lifeline.

00:20:59.645 --> 00:21:02.675
The mines employed not just miners,

00:21:02.985 --> 00:21:06.225
but safety deputies, whose job was

00:21:06.255 --> 00:21:08.555
to inspect the mines for gas leaks,

00:21:08.885 --> 00:21:11.485
structural problems, and other dangers.

00:21:12.135 --> 00:21:15.495
Without them, no mine could legally open.

00:21:16.365 --> 00:21:19.785
Their union, which represented around

00:21:19.785 --> 00:21:24.378
17,000 workers, voted in October 1984

00:21:24.822 --> 00:21:27.945
to strike in sympathy with the miners.

00:21:28.975 --> 00:21:30.465
This would have been

00:21:30.475 --> 00:21:32.395
game over for Thatcher.

00:21:33.005 --> 00:21:35.085
Every pit in Britain would have

00:21:35.085 --> 00:21:37.295
been forced to close immediately,

00:21:37.675 --> 00:21:39.865
regardless of whether the miners

00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:42.165
inside wanted to work or not.

00:21:43.035 --> 00:21:46.075
But at the last minute, the government

00:21:46.265 --> 00:21:49.805
made a small concession on the pit closure

00:21:49.835 --> 00:21:52.885
process, just enough for the deputies to

00:21:52.945 --> 00:21:55.445
call off the strike and accept a deal.

00:21:56.415 --> 00:21:58.395
The lifeline was gone.

00:21:59.688 --> 00:22:03.778
On the 3rd of March, 1985, almost exactly

00:22:03.778 --> 00:22:06.858
one year after the strike had begun, there

00:22:06.858 --> 00:22:09.688
was a vote on whether to return to work.

00:22:10.378 --> 00:22:14.992
It was close, but “yes” won, 98 to 91.

00:22:15.708 --> 00:22:17.028
There was no deal.

00:22:17.608 --> 00:22:19.948
There were no concessions.

00:22:20.558 --> 00:22:23.528
The miners went back with nothing.

00:22:24.908 --> 00:22:28.338
At many pits, they marched back with brass

00:22:28.338 --> 00:22:31.008
bands playing and union banners flying.

00:22:31.618 --> 00:22:33.388
It was a display of dignity in

00:22:33.388 --> 00:22:35.608
defeat that moved even many of

00:22:35.608 --> 00:22:37.578
those who had opposed the strike.

00:22:38.868 --> 00:22:41.458
Scargill told reporters the campaign

00:22:41.458 --> 00:22:43.318
against closures would continue.

00:22:44.298 --> 00:22:46.388
Margaret Thatcher said she was

00:22:46.398 --> 00:22:48.588
relieved the miners would be returning

00:22:48.588 --> 00:22:51.668
to work, but made no attempt to

00:22:51.668 --> 00:22:53.858
disguise that her government had won.

00:22:54.498 --> 00:22:57.888
What followed over the next ten years was

00:22:57.918 --> 00:23:00.828
precisely what Scargill had predicted, and

00:23:00.838 --> 00:23:03.598
precisely what the government had denied.

00:23:04.878 --> 00:23:07.028
At the time of the strike, there were

00:23:07.078 --> 00:23:10.118
170 operating coal mines in Britain.

00:23:11.058 --> 00:23:14.728
By 1994, only 15 remained.

00:23:15.318 --> 00:23:19.428
Nearly 90% of the workforce had been shed.

00:23:20.608 --> 00:23:22.418
The final deep coal mine

00:23:22.418 --> 00:23:25.618
in Britain closed in 2015.

00:23:27.178 --> 00:23:29.638
For the communities built around those

00:23:29.658 --> 00:23:34.028
mines, the consequences were severe and in

00:23:34.028 --> 00:23:36.928
many cases have never fully been reversed.

00:23:37.908 --> 00:23:40.638
If you look at a map of unemployment

00:23:40.638 --> 00:23:42.688
and economic deprivation in the

00:23:42.688 --> 00:23:45.378
UK, many of the most affected areas

00:23:45.728 --> 00:23:47.748
are in former mining communities.

00:23:48.498 --> 00:23:50.678
The mine had been practically the

00:23:50.678 --> 00:23:53.248
only reliable employment opportunity,

00:23:53.718 --> 00:23:56.848
and with it gone, there was nothing.

00:23:57.658 --> 00:23:59.598
In most former mining communities,

00:23:59.948 --> 00:24:02.678
nothing comparable came to replace it.

00:24:03.918 --> 00:24:05.888
And of course, the social

00:24:05.918 --> 00:24:08.878
consequences were real and lasting.

00:24:09.678 --> 00:24:11.498
Mental health problems from

00:24:11.498 --> 00:24:13.088
people struggling with the

00:24:13.268 --> 00:24:15.058
loss of their identity, their

00:24:15.098 --> 00:24:17.098
purpose, and their community.

00:24:17.648 --> 00:24:19.818
Many areas saw increases in

00:24:19.818 --> 00:24:22.768
crime, alcohol and drug abuse.

00:24:23.468 --> 00:24:25.538
Poverty became entrenched.

00:24:26.218 --> 00:24:30.268
And entire generations grew up knowing

00:24:30.278 --> 00:24:32.538
what had happened, and pointing the

00:24:32.538 --> 00:24:35.508
finger squarely at Margaret Thatcher.

00:24:36.862 --> 00:24:39.702
And this has had a long-term impact

00:24:39.962 --> 00:24:42.352
on the political geography of Britain.

00:24:43.602 --> 00:24:45.992
The Conservative Party was already

00:24:46.012 --> 00:24:48.572
not particularly popular in northern

00:24:48.572 --> 00:24:52.412
England, and this episode made it toxic.

00:24:53.262 --> 00:24:55.932
In many northern cities and towns,

00:24:56.312 --> 00:24:59.132
voting Conservative became almost

00:24:59.462 --> 00:25:03.582
unthinkable, and to this day, many people

00:25:03.822 --> 00:25:05.812
simply cannot forgive the Conservative

00:25:05.812 --> 00:25:08.322
Party for what they did to the miners.

00:25:09.392 --> 00:25:11.712
So, who was right?

00:25:12.232 --> 00:25:14.252
Well, Thatcher's defenders

00:25:14.502 --> 00:25:16.402
would point out several things.

00:25:17.142 --> 00:25:19.632
The coal industry was not economically

00:25:19.642 --> 00:25:21.792
viable in its existing form.

00:25:22.482 --> 00:25:24.392
The subsidies required to keep these

00:25:24.402 --> 00:25:27.332
loss-making pits open were enormous,

00:25:27.672 --> 00:25:30.692
and the coal produced was more expensive

00:25:30.962 --> 00:25:32.842
than that available from other sources.

00:25:33.812 --> 00:25:35.562
Britain's industrial structure

00:25:35.982 --> 00:25:38.252
needed to change, and had

00:25:38.292 --> 00:25:40.522
been changing since the 1950s.

00:25:41.212 --> 00:25:43.542
The coal industry did need to go,

00:25:44.082 --> 00:25:46.392
and this is before we get to any

00:25:46.392 --> 00:25:48.452
kind of environmental discussion.

00:25:49.572 --> 00:25:51.282
Her supporters would also

00:25:51.282 --> 00:25:53.232
point to Scargill's tactics.

00:25:53.832 --> 00:25:56.242
He called a national strike without

00:25:56.282 --> 00:25:58.642
a public vote, which split the

00:25:58.642 --> 00:26:00.522
union and gave the government

00:26:00.602 --> 00:26:02.482
a crucial political advantage.

00:26:03.092 --> 00:26:06.192
He started the strike in spring, the worst

00:26:06.202 --> 00:26:09.502
possible time, when coal demand is lowest.

00:26:10.302 --> 00:26:12.692
He attempted to use flying pickets to

00:26:12.722 --> 00:26:15.242
intimidate miners who had exercised

00:26:15.242 --> 00:26:17.802
their democratic right not to strike.

00:26:18.902 --> 00:26:21.092
His refusal to compromise at

00:26:21.092 --> 00:26:23.182
various points when deals might

00:26:23.182 --> 00:26:24.942
still have been possible is

00:26:24.942 --> 00:26:27.292
still a source of bitter debate.

00:26:28.102 --> 00:26:30.372
Those on the other side will point

00:26:30.382 --> 00:26:32.862
to the secrecy and the scale of

00:26:32.862 --> 00:26:34.652
the government's real intentions.

00:26:35.442 --> 00:26:37.522
Cabinet papers confirmed that

00:26:37.552 --> 00:26:39.202
the government and MacGregor

00:26:39.562 --> 00:26:41.672
planned to close far more pits

00:26:41.962 --> 00:26:43.602
than they ever admitted publicly,

00:26:44.022 --> 00:26:46.492
and that they knew the employment

00:26:46.512 --> 00:26:49.002
consequences would be devastating.

00:26:49.902 --> 00:26:52.512
The conduct at Orgreave, and the

00:26:52.522 --> 00:26:54.982
subsequent cover-up by police, was

00:26:54.982 --> 00:26:57.652
a serious miscarriage of justice.

00:26:59.088 --> 00:27:01.948
Most importantly, perhaps, they will argue

00:27:02.158 --> 00:27:04.248
that the government's victory was not

00:27:04.248 --> 00:27:07.438
followed by any serious attempt to help

00:27:07.668 --> 00:27:09.778
the communities that had been destroyed.

00:27:10.498 --> 00:27:12.988
No major investment programme,

00:27:13.088 --> 00:27:14.928
no retraining at scale,

00:27:14.938 --> 00:27:17.118
no replacement industries.

00:27:17.338 --> 00:27:20.718
Just closure, and silence.

00:27:21.858 --> 00:27:24.578
So, to wrap things up, the miners'

00:27:24.588 --> 00:27:27.388
strike was not just a labour dispute.

00:27:27.718 --> 00:27:30.288
It was a defining confrontation about

00:27:30.418 --> 00:27:33.888
who held power in Britain, and about

00:27:33.888 --> 00:27:36.218
what happens to people when the economic

00:27:36.228 --> 00:27:39.058
foundation of their lives is removed.

00:27:40.078 --> 00:27:41.978
Margaret Thatcher won.

00:27:42.638 --> 00:27:43.718
The miners lost.

00:27:44.438 --> 00:27:46.948
And in some of the places where those

00:27:46.978 --> 00:27:49.598
miners once worked and lived, the effects

00:27:49.638 --> 00:27:52.808
of that defeat are still being felt today.

00:27:54.818 --> 00:27:56.948
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:27:56.958 --> 00:27:59.118
episode on the miners' strike.

00:27:59.448 --> 00:28:00.728
I hope it's been an interesting one,

00:28:01.048 --> 00:28:02.558
and that you have learnt something new.

00:28:03.208 --> 00:28:05.298
I’d love to know…had you heard

00:28:05.328 --> 00:28:06.638
of the miners’ strike before?

00:28:07.108 --> 00:28:09.438
Have you been to any of these areas

00:28:09.438 --> 00:28:12.108
of the UK that were the most affected?

00:28:12.538 --> 00:28:14.418
And are there similar episodes

00:28:14.458 --> 00:28:15.768
in your country’s history?

00:28:16.298 --> 00:28:18.318
I would love to know, so let’s

00:28:18.318 --> 00:28:19.708
get this discussion started.

00:28:19.968 --> 00:28:21.988
The place for that is our community forum,

00:28:22.298 --> 00:28:24.958
which is at community.leonardoenglish.com.

00:28:25.678 --> 00:28:27.888
You've been listening to English Learning

00:28:27.888 --> 00:28:30.568
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:28:30.998 --> 00:28:33.908
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:28:33.918 --> 00:28:36.228
I'll catch you in the next episode.

